===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|acre|pl2qual=UK colloquial}}

# An [[English]] [[unit]] of [[land]] [[area]] ([[symbol]]: '''a.''' or '''ac.''') [[originally]] [[denoting]] a [[day]]'s [[ploughing]] for a [[yoke]] of [[oxen]], [[now]] [[standardize]]d as 4,840 [[square yard]]s or 4,046.86 [[square meter]]s.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=w:Edwin Black
|title=Internal Combustion
|chapter=2|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL4103950W
|passage=Buried within the Mediterranean littoral are some seventy to ninety million tons of slag from ancient smelting, about a third of it concentrated in Iberia. This ceaseless industrial fueling caused the deforestation of an estimated fifty to seventy million '''acres''' of woodlands.}}
## {{lb|en|Chester|historical}} An area of 10,240 square yards or 4 [[quarter]]s.<ref name="Holland"/>
# Any of various similar units of area in other [[system]]s.
# {{lb|en|informal|usually|in the plural}} A wide expanse.
#: {{ux|en|I like my new house - there’s '''acres''' of space!}}
# {{lb|en|informal|usually|in the plural}} A large quantity.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[field]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The [[acre's breadth]] by the [[acre's length|length]], [[English]] [[unit]]s of [[length]] [[equal]] to the [[statute]] [[dimension]]s of the [[acre]]: 22 yds (≈20 m) by 220 yds (≈200 m).
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[duel]] [[fought]] between [[individual]] [[Scot]]s and [[Englishmen]] in the [[borderland]]s.

